# Fathom — Movement and controls

This file defines the simulation's fixed timestep, how the forager and the
predators travel the maze, and the controls a player operates the game with. The
maze the bodies travel is in `specs/maze.md`, each predator's own behavior is in
`specs/predators.md`, and what the sonar pulse and the ink do is in
`specs/sensing.md`. Every figure below carries the name this specification gives
it.

## The fixed-step core

The simulation runs on a fixed timestep of `TICK_HZ` (`120`) steps per second, so
one simulation tick, `TICK_DT`, is exactly `1/120 s`. Every rate in this
specification is integrated in whole ticks of that length.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The core is decoupled from rendering. A frame reports how much time has elapsed
and the simulation advances by the whole `TICK_DT` ticks that elapsed time
completes, carrying the remainder into the next frame, so the number of ticks run
over an interval of game time is the same however that interval was divided into
frames.
{{else}}
The core is decoupled from rendering. The engine hands each frame a delta time and
the simulation advances by the whole `TICK_DT` ticks that delta completes,
carrying the remainder into the next frame, so the number of ticks run over an
interval of game time is the same however that interval was divided into frames.
{{/if}}

The tick is the game's whole clock. Movement, cooldowns, timers and the creatures'
decisions all advance inside it, so the same starting state driven by the same
inputs over the same elapsed game time reaches the same state every time.

## Where a body may travel

The forager and every predator are bodies on the tile grid, and each one travels
along the center lines of corridors, always along one of the four cardinal
directions or at rest. Rock is solid to every body. A body's center stays on
corridor tiles, so it enters a tile only when that tile is open to it, and a body
standing on a tile whose neighbors are all closed to it stays where it stands.

The den gate and the den interior are open to predators alone. A predator crosses
the gate leaving the den and returning to it, and the forager's travel is over
corridor tiles, so the forager stands neither on the gate tile nor inside the den
chamber.

Motion is continuous rather than tile-by-tile: a body slides between tile centers
at its own speed, and the centers are the points at which it may change direction.

## The forager's travel

The forager travels at `FORAGER_SPEED` (`128` logical units per second, four tiles
per second), and that speed is constant wherever it is in the maze. It travels
while a movement action is held and comes to rest where it stands when none is
held, which is what `moving` in `specs/state.md` reports.

The predators travel at their own speeds, which `specs/predators.md` and the
per-kind files fix.

## Turning

A movement action sets the forager's **desired direction**, and that direction
holds until another movement action replaces it. The desired direction is a
request, and where it is honored depends on how it stands to the current heading.

| Desired direction | Where it is honored |
| --- | --- |
| The opposite of the current heading | At once, wherever the forager stands. |
| Perpendicular to the current heading | At the next tile center the forager reaches, if the tile that way is open to it. |

At a tile center the forager takes the desired direction when the tile that way is
open to it. Otherwise it carries on along its current heading while the tile ahead
is open, and comes to rest at the center when that tile is closed to it. A forager
at rest takes the desired direction when the tile that way is open to it, and
stays at rest otherwise.

The desired direction is buffered, so a direction set slightly before a junction is
still the desired direction when the junction's center arrives, and the forager
takes the turn there. A turn onto a perpendicular direction is taken at a tile
center and nowhere else.

## The wrap tunnel

The two mouths of the wrap tunnel `specs/maze.md` defines are neighboring tiles,
and a body crosses between them exactly as it crosses between any other pair of
neighbors, on the terms that file fixes. Travel through the tunnel is one
continuous move, entered by traveling into a mouth and left by traveling on out
of the other.

## Controls

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Keyboard only. This game stands on no engine, so the input layer beneath the game
is yours to write, and what the game reads from that layer is the actions below.
`ACTIONS` holds their names, in the order given here, and `BINDINGS` maps each
action to the `KeyboardEvent.code` values that fire it, so a binding is a physical
key rather than a layout-dependent character.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Keyboard only, through actions the game registers with the engine. The actions are
the vocabulary of the `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4-two-buttons`) touch layout the engine is
built with: register exactly the ten names in `ACTIONS`, in the order given here,
each bound to the keys `BINDINGS` gives it. The engine owns the keyboard and
resolves each action, so the game reads the actions rather than key events. The
keys are `KeyboardEvent.code` values, so a binding is a physical key rather than a
layout-dependent character.
{{else}}
Keyboard only, through actions the game registers with the engine. The actions are
the vocabulary of the `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4-two-buttons`) touch layout the engine is
built with: register exactly the ten names in `ACTIONS`, in the order given here,
each bound to the keys `BINDINGS` gives it. The engine owns the keyboard and
resolves each action, and an action is read only through a player controller, in
that controller's tick. The keys are `KeyboardEvent.code` values, so a binding is a
physical key rather than a layout-dependent character.
{{/if}}

| Action | Keys | Does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `up` | `ArrowUp`, `KeyW` | Sets the desired direction up. Moves a menu selection up. |
| `down` | `ArrowDown`, `KeyS` | Sets the desired direction down. Moves a menu selection down. |
| `left` | `ArrowLeft`, `KeyA` | Sets the desired direction left. |
| `right` | `ArrowRight`, `KeyD` | Sets the desired direction right. |
| `a` | `Space` | Emits a sonar pulse. |
| `b` | `ShiftLeft`, `ShiftRight` | Releases an ink cloud. |
| `confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` | Accepts the highlighted menu item. |
| `back` | `Escape` | Leaves the current screen. |
| `pause` | `Escape`, `KeyP` | Pauses live play. |
| `mute` | `KeyM` | Toggles the game's sound. |

### Where each action is read

`Space` fires `a` and `confirm`, and `Escape` fires `back` and `pause`. Each screen
reads the actions in its own row and leaves the rest alone, so one key does one
thing on any given screen.

| Screen | Actions read |
| --- | --- |
| `"playing"` | The four movement actions, held; `a`; `b`; `pause`; `mute`. |
| `"title"`, `"howto"`, `"paused"`, `"gameover"` | `up` and `down`, as press edges; `confirm`; `back`; `mute`. |
| `"countdown"`, `"cleared"` | `mute`. |

The four movement actions are read as held values, so the forager travels while
one of them is held. Every other action is read once per press.

`specs/ui.md` defines where each menu selection and confirmation leads,
`specs/sensing.md` defines what the sonar pulse and the ink cloud do, and
`specs/progression.md` defines what muting silences.
